6.text:
In the one parameter family

$$C_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{\,n}\qquad E\in {\mathbf C}$$
the Gauss-Manin connection splits under the involution $q\mapsto -q$.
For $n=2k$ the $(-1)$-eigenspace is of dimension $k-1$ and the
$+1$-eigenspace is of dimension $\displaystyle g_{k}=\Big[\frac{k-1}{2}
\Big]$.  In particular on the $-$ eigenspace

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}\;{\cal H}^{1}_{-}(C_{E})=2g(C_{E})-2g(D_{E})=2k-2-
2\Big[\frac{k-1}2\Big]=k-1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that the real period
\begin{equation*}
T_{k}(E)=\oint_{{\gamma}_{-}}\frac{dq}{\sqrt{\,2E-q^{2}+q^{2k}\,}}
\end{equation*}
satisfies a linear differential equation of order
\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2k)=k-1=\frac{n}{2}-1 ,\qquad {\rm rk}\;{\cal H}^{1}(C_{E})=
2k-2=n-2,
\end{equation*}
whereas for $n=2k+1$

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}\;{\cal H}^{1}(C_{E})=2g(C_{E})=n-1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence $u_{r}(n)=n-1$ for odd $n$ and $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ for the even
$n>2$.  Up to the trivial free case $n=2$ the first two resonant
even numbers are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
and indeed $u_{r}(n_{i})=n_{i}/2-1$ while
$u_{r}(n_{i}\pm1)=n_{i}\pm1-1$.  Putting $m=n_{1}/2=2$ we obtain on the
$(-1)$–eigenspace the normal form

\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}(p,q)=\frac12\Big(p^{2}+q^{2}
-\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}\,q^{\,m}\Big)
 =\frac12\Big(p^{2}+\omega^{2}q^{2}\Big) ,
\end{equation*}
with

\begin{equation*}
\omega^{2}=1-\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}
      =1-\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
In the usual $2\pi$–normalisation the hypergeometric period is

\begin{equation*}
{\mathfrak T}(\alpha)=
\frac{2\pi}{\omega(\alpha)}\;{}_{2}F_{1}\!\Big(\tfrac12,
 \tfrac1m;\tfrac{1+1}{m};\alpha\Big), \qquad {\mathfrak T}(0)=2\pi,
\end{equation*}
which for $m=2$ collapses to the elementary harmonic oscillator
${\mathfrak T}(\alpha)=2\pi(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}:=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $2\pi$–normalisation

\begin{equation*}
 {\mathfrak T}(\alpha_{0})=
\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}=2\pi\sqrt{2}\approx 8.89,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential factor $2\pi$ the normalised real
hypergeometric period is

\[
T_{{\rm norm}}(\alpha_{0})=\frac{{\mathfrak T}(\alpha_{0})}{2\pi}
      =(1-\alpha_{0})^{-1/2}=\sqrt{2},
\]

which is the familiar harmonic value $\omega^{-1}=\sqrt{2}$.
In the often quoted $\pi$–normalisation this is $T(\tfrac12)=\pi\sqrt2$,
the elementary limit of the ${}_{2}F_{1}$–period.  Discarding the
$2\pi$–factor this is the usual statement $T(1/2)=\sqrt2$; with the
physical period the value is $2\pi\sqrt2$.  This is the $n_{1}=4$,
$n_{2}=6$ resonant case; with the trivial free case removed the
non-trivial first resonant pair is $n_{1}=6$, $n_{2}=10$, i.e.
$\alpha_{0}=5/10=1/2$ again, the same harmonic limit. ∎

