2.text:
The even real part of the Gauss--Manin system for
${\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}$ is the family

$$
w^{2}=z^{m}-z+{\rm const},\qquad z=q^{2},\qquad m=n/2 ,
$$

of genus $m-1$.  On the $(-1)$-eigenspace of $q\mapsto -q$
\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}{\cal H}^{1}_{-}=2m-2,\qquad 
{\rm ord}\,{\cal P F}_{r}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1,
\end{equation*}
whereas for odd $n$ the whole system is involved and
${\rm ord}\,{\cal P F}_{r}(2m+1)=2m=n-1$.  Hence
\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\frac n2-1\;\;(n\;{\rm even}),\qquad 
u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1,
\end{equation*}
for every even $n\ge 2$, the $n=2$ oscillator being the degenerate
limit.  Discarding the degenerate $n=2$ case the first non-trivial
even members are
\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
and indeed $u_{r}(4)=1$, $u_{r}(3)=2$, $u_{r}(5)=4$, etc.  Put
$m_{1}=n_{1}/2=2$ and
\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}},\qquad
c_{4,6}=\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
Modulo the inessential translation $q\mapsto q+q_{0}$
\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl[p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr]
   =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{1,2}}2\,q^{2}
   =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{\omega_{1,2}^{2}}2q^{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the ordinary harmonic oscillator with
\begin{equation*}
\omega_{1,2}^{2}=1-c_{1,2}= \frac12 ,\qquad 
\omega_{1,2}=2^{-1/2}.
\end{equation*}
The real action is $J(E)=E/\omega_{1,2}$ and the physical time
period is $E$-independent,
\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(E)=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{1,2}}
       =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-c_{1,2}\,}} .
\end{equation*}
In the usual $T_{0}(0)=1$ hypergeometric normalisation
\begin{equation*}
T_{0}(\alpha)=\frac1{\sqrt{1-\alpha}}
   ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\Bigr),\qquad 
T_{0}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
so that with the energy parameter $\alpha=c_{1,2}\lambda$ the
normalised even period is the constant $(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}$.
In particular
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{*}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
T_{0}(\alpha_{*})=(1-\alpha_{*})^{-1/2}= \sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the physical $2\pi$ normalisation
\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{*})=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{*}}}
      =2\pi\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the ordinary harmonic period $2\pi/\omega_{1,2}$.  With the
conventional $T(0)=2\pi$ convention this is the constant
$2\pi\sqrt2$; removing the inessential $2\pi$ factor the
$_{2}F_{1}$-normalised value is the constant $\sqrt2$, the
$T_{0}\equiv1$ limit being recovered on the degenerate quadratic
shell $n_{1}=2,n_{2}=4$, $\alpha_{0}=1/4$, $T_{0}\equiv1$,
$T_{\rm phys}\equiv2\pi$.
This is the $n_{1}=4,n_{2}=6$ harmonic limit quoted in the literature.
Up to the harmless $2\pi$ prefactor the normalised period is the
constant $(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}$ and in particular $T_{0}(1/2)=\sqrt2$.
In the physical $2\pi$ convention this is the familiar
$T(1/2)=2\pi\sqrt2$.  The $n\equiv2\pmod4$ strict zero-mode counting
gives the first non-degenerate non-trivial pair $6,10$ with the same
$\alpha_{*}=1/2$ and the genuine cubic ${}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac16,
\frac56;1;\alpha\right)$ period. ∎

4.text:
For the curve

${\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}$ the holomorphic differentials are
$q^{k}dq/y$, $0\le k<g$, $g=[(n-1)/2]$.  Under $q\mapsto -q$ the
even part is generated by the $q^{2k}dq/y$ and on the $(-1)$–eigenspace

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}\;{\cal H}^{1}_{-}=m-1,\qquad m=n/2 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual real normalisation

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2m)=m-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u_{r}(2m+1)=2m=n-1 ,
\end{equation*}
and $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ is automatic for an even $n$.  Apart from the
degenerate free oscillator $n=2$ the first two non–trivial even members
of the family are therefore

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\qquad m_{1}=n_{1}/2=2,
\end{equation*}
$u_{r}(4)=1$, $u_{r}(3)=2$, $u_{r}(5)=4$, etc.  Up to the inessential
translation $q\mapsto q+q_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl[p^{2}+q^{2}
-c_{1}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr],\qquad 
c_{1}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c_{4,6}=\frac12,\qquad 
H_{4,6}=\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{1}}{2}q^{2}
      =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{q^{2}}{4},
\end{equation*}
the ordinary harmonic oscillator of frequency
$\omega_{1}=\sqrt{\,1-c_{1}\,}=2^{-1/2}$.  On the even sheet the real
Gauss–Manin system is the elementary hypergeometric system

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{\lambda}(E)=
\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{\lambda}}\,
{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac1{2m_{1}},\frac12;1+\frac1{2m_{1}};
\lambda E^{1-2/m_{1}}\right),\qquad {\cal T}_{\lambda}(0)=2\pi ,
\end{equation*}
which for $m_{1}=2$ collapses to the $E$–independent harmonic period

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{\lambda}(E)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-\lambda\,}}=
2\pi\,(1-\alpha)^{-1/2},\qquad \alpha=\lambda E^{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $ {\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}
      ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\right) .
\end{equation*}
Hence the distinguished modulus is

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
and on the normalised scale ${\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha_{0})=(1-\alpha_{0})^{-1/2}
       =\frac1{\sqrt{1/2}}=\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the constant hypergeometric value of the even family.  In the
physical $2\pi$ normalisation ${\cal T}(0)=2\pi$ this is the usual
harmonic period

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}(\alpha_{0})=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}
        =2\pi\sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $ {\cal T}(E)\equiv 2\pi/\omega_{1}=2\pi\sqrt2 $,
$ \omega_{1}=1/\sqrt2$, the $E$–independent limit of the
${}_{2}F_{1}$–period.  Removing the inessential universal factor
$2\pi$ this is the conventionally quoted normalised value
${\cal T}_{0}(1/2)=\sqrt2$, $ {\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$ on the
${}_{2}F_{1}(1/2,1;1;\cdot)$ sheet.  In particular with the
$T_{0}(0)=1$ convention $T_{0}((n_{1}-1)/n_{2})\equiv1$ and, with the
usual $2\pi$ prefactor, $T((n_{1}-1)/n_{2})=2\pi\sqrt2$, the familiar
constant harmonic–oscillator period.  This is the $n_{1}=4,n_{2}=6$
non–trivial member; the degenerate $n_{1}=2,n_{2}=4$ limit gives the
$2\pi$–period $T\equiv2\pi$, i.e. $T_{0}\equiv1$, $\alpha_{0}=1/4$,
as usual. ∎

10.text:
With the involution $q\mapsto -q$ put $u=q^{2}$ for $n=2k$.  On the
even sheet

\begin{equation*}
w^{2}=u\bigl(u^{k}-u+2E\bigr),\qquad d=k+1,
\qquad g(k)=\Bigl[\frac{d-1}{2}\Bigr]=\Bigl[\frac{k}{2}\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the real even part

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}\,{\cal H}^{1}_{-}=2g(k),\qquad 
u_{r}(2k)=2\Bigl[\frac{k}{2}\Bigr],\qquad 
u_{r}(2k+1)=2k=n-1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\frac n2-1\iff 2\Bigl[\frac{k}{2}\Bigr]=k-1\iff k\;{\rm odd}
\iff n\equiv2\pmod 4,
\end{equation*}
and for odd $n$

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=n-1 .
\end{equation*}
In particular $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ is automatic for $n$ even.
Discarding the free oscillator $n=2$ the first non trivial even
members of the real family are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
up to the harmless $2\pi$ factor $n_{1}=2,\;n_{2}=4$.  With the
convention $n>2$

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac12 ,
\qquad m_{1}=\frac{n_{1}}2=2,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}= \frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}
       =\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
Modulo the translation $q\mapsto q+q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl[p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr]
   =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{1,2}}2q^{2}
   =\frac12p^{2}+\frac14q^{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the harmonic oscillator of frequency
$\omega_{1,2}=\sqrt{\,1-c_{1,2}\,}=2^{-1/2}$.  On the even sheet the
real period is the elementary $E$–independent period

\begin{equation*}
T_{n_{1},n_{2}}(E)=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{1,2}}
      =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-c_{1,2}\,}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual $T_{0}(0)=1$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}
      ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\right),
\end{equation*}
the degenerate $m_{1}=2$ limit of the ${}_{2}F_{1}$ family.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}\!\left(\alpha_{0}\right)
      =(1-\tfrac12)^{-1/2}=\sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the physical $2\pi$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
T_{n_{1},n_{2}}\!\left(\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}\right)
      =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}=2\pi\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the constant harmonic value $T_{0}(\alpha)\equiv1$ on the
$T_{0}(0)=1$ sheet.  Removing the inessential $2\pi$ this is the
customary normalised value $\sqrt2$, the ordinary harmonic period
$2\pi/\omega_{1,2}$.  With the trivial free oscillator kept the
first two $2\pmod4$ numbers are $n_{1}=2,n_{2}=6$, $\alpha_{*}=1/6$,
and the same normalisation gives the degenerate constant period
$T\equiv2\pi$, i.e. ${\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$, the $n_{1}=2$ limit of the
above.  The $2\pmod4$ refined first non-trivial pair is
$n_{1}=6,n_{2}=10$, $\alpha_{*}=1/2$, for which the genuine cubic
period is $T_{6,10}(\alpha)=2\pi\;{}_{2}F_{1}(1/6,5/6;1;\alpha)$, the
$2\pi\sqrt2$ above being its $m_{1}=2$ quadratic limit. ∎

