-c_{1}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr],\qquad 
c_{1}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c_{4,6}=\frac12,\qquad 
H_{4,6}=\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{1}}{2}q^{2}
      =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{q^{2}}{4},
\end{equation*}
the ordinary harmonic oscillator of frequency
$\omega_{1}=\sqrt{\,1-c_{1}\,}=2^{-1/2}$.  On the even sheet the real
Gauss–Manin system is the elementary hypergeometric system

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{\lambda}(E)=
\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{\lambda}}\,
{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac1{2m_{1}},\frac12;1+\frac1{2m_{1}};
\lambda E^{1-2/m_{1}}\right),\qquad {\cal T}_{\lambda}(0)=2\pi ,
\end{equation*}
which for $m_{1}=2$ collapses to the $E$–independent harmonic period

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{\lambda}(E)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-\lambda\,}}=
2\pi\,(1-\alpha)^{-1/2},\qquad \alpha=\lambda E^{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the $ {\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}
      ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\right) .
\end{equation*}
Hence the distinguished modulus is

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
and on the normalised scale ${\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha_{0})=(1-\alpha_{0})^{-1/2}
       =\frac1{\sqrt{1/2}}=\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the constant hypergeometric value of the even family.  In the
physical $2\pi$ normalisation ${\cal T}(0)=2\pi$ this is the usual
harmonic period

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}(\alpha_{0})=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}
        =2\pi\sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $ {\cal T}(E)\equiv 2\pi/\omega_{1}=2\pi\sqrt2 $,
$ \omega_{1}=1/\sqrt2$, the $E$–independent limit of the
${}_{2}F_{1}$–period.  Removing the inessential universal factor
$2\pi$ this is the conventionally quoted normalised value
${\cal T}_{0}(1/2)=\sqrt2$, $ {\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$ on the
${}_{2}F_{1}(1/2,1;1;\cdot)$ sheet.  In particular with the
$T_{0}(0)=1$ convention $T_{0}((n_{1}-1)/n_{2})\equiv1$ and, with the
usual $2\pi$ prefactor, $T((n_{1}-1)/n_{2})=2\pi\sqrt2$, the familiar
constant harmonic–oscillator period.  This is the $n_{1}=4,n_{2}=6$
non–trivial member; the degenerate $n_{1}=2,n_{2}=4$ limit gives the
$2\pi$–period $T\equiv2\pi$, i.e. $T_{0}\equiv1$, $\alpha_{0}=1/4$,
as usual. ∎

public_text.10:
With the involution $q\mapsto -q$ put $u=q^{2}$ for $n=2k$.  On the
even sheet

\begin{equation*}
w^{2}=u\bigl(u^{k}-u+2E\bigr),\qquad d=k+1,
\qquad g(k)=\Bigl[\frac{d-1}{2}\Bigr]=\Bigl[\frac{k}{2}\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the real even part

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}\,{\cal H}^{1}_{-}=2g(k),\qquad 
u_{r}(2k)=2\Bigl[\frac{k}{2}\Bigr],\qquad 
u_{r}(2k+1)=2k=n-1 .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=\frac n2-1\iff 2\Bigl[\frac{k}{2}\Bigr]=k-1\iff k\;{\rm odd}
\iff n\equiv2\pmod 4,
\end{equation*}
and for odd $n$

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(n)=n-1 .
\end{equation*}
In particular $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ is automatic for $n$ even.
Discarding the free oscillator $n=2$ the first non trivial even
members of the real family are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
up to the harmless $2\pi$ factor $n_{1}=2,\;n_{2}=4$.  With the
convention $n>2$

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{0}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{3}{6}=\frac12 ,
\qquad m_{1}=\frac{n_{1}}2=2,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}= \frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}
       =\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
Modulo the translation $q\mapsto q+q_{0}$,

\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl[p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr]
   =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{1,2}}2q^{2}
   =\frac12p^{2}+\frac14q^{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the harmonic oscillator of frequency
$\omega_{1,2}=\sqrt{\,1-c_{1,2}\,}=2^{-1/2}$.  On the even sheet the
real period is the elementary $E$–independent period

\begin{equation*}
T_{n_{1},n_{2}}(E)=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{1,2}}
      =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-c_{1,2}\,}}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual $T_{0}(0)=1$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}
      ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\right),
\end{equation*}
the degenerate $m_{1}=2$ limit of the ${}_{2}F_{1}$ family.  In
particular

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}\!\left(\alpha_{0}\right)
      =(1-\tfrac12)^{-1/2}=\sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the physical $2\pi$ normalisation

\begin{equation*}
T_{n_{1},n_{2}}\!\left(\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}\right)
      =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{0}}}=2\pi\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the constant harmonic value $T_{0}(\alpha)\equiv1$ on the
$T_{0}(0)=1$ sheet.  Removing the inessential $2\pi$ this is the
customary normalised value $\sqrt2$, the ordinary harmonic period
$2\pi/\omega_{1,2}$.  With the trivial free oscillator kept the
first two $2\pmod4$ numbers are $n_{1}=2,n_{2}=6$, $\alpha_{*}=1/6$,
and the same normalisation gives the degenerate constant period
$T\equiv2\pi$, i.e. ${\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$, the $n_{1}=2$ limit of the
above.  The $2\pmod4$ refined first non-trivial pair is
$n_{1}=6,n_{2}=10$, $\alpha_{*}=1/2$, for which the genuine cubic
period is $T_{6,10}(\alpha)=2\pi\;{}_{2}F_{1}(1/6,5/6;1;\alpha)$, the
$2\pi\sqrt2$ above being its $m_{1}=2$ quadratic limit. ∎

12.text:
For the real curve ${\cal C}_{E}\!:\;y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}$ put $u=q^{2}$.
On the $-1$ eigenspace of $q\mapsto -q$

\begin{equation*}
w^{2}=u^{k}-u+2E,\qquad k={n}/{2},\qquad 
{\rm rk}\,{\cal H}^{1}_{-}=2k-2 ,
\end{equation*}
and with the real normalisation

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2k)=k-1=\frac n2-1 ,\qquad u_{r}(2k+1)=2k=n-1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that 
$u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ and $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ for every even $n$.
Discarding the degenerate free oscillator $n=2$ the first non-trivial
even members are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6 ,
\end{equation*}
$u_{r}(4)=1$, $u_{r}(3)=2$, $u_{r}(5)=4$, etc., and with the
trivial free limit $n_{1}^{0}=2$, $n_{2}^{0}=4$.
Put $m_{1}=n_{1}/2$ and

\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}= \frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}\;,
\qquad H_{n_{1},n_{2}}=\frac12\Bigl[p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
For the first non-trivial pair

\begin{equation*}
c_{4,6}=\frac{2}{4}\sqrt{\frac{2}{2}}=\frac12,\qquad
m_{1}=2,
\end{equation*}
and modulo the harmless translation $q\mapsto q+q_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
H_{4,6}(p,q)=\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{4,6}}{2}q^{2}
          =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{q^{2}}{4},
\qquad 
\omega_{4,6}=\sqrt{\,1-c_{4,6}\,}=2^{-1/2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the ordinary harmonic oscillator.  On the even sheet the
Gauss–Manin system is the elementary ${}_{2}F_{1}$-limit

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}
={}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\Bigr),\qquad {\cal T}_{0}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the physical $2\pi$ normalisation
${\mathfrak T}(\alpha)=2\pi{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)$,

\begin{equation*}
{\mathfrak T}_{n_{1},n_{2}}(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi}
{\sqrt{\,1-\alpha\,}},\qquad {\mathfrak T}_{n_{1},n_{2}}(0)=2\pi .
\end{equation*}

The distinguished modulus is

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{*}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12 ,
\end{equation*}
and on the ${\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$ sheet

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha_{*})=(1-\alpha_{*})^{-1/2}
       =\bigl(1-\tfrac12\bigr)^{-1/2}=\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the $m_{1}=2$ degenerate value
${}_{2}F_{1}(\tfrac12,1;1;\tfrac12)=\sqrt2$.  In the usual
$2\pi$ physical normalisation the real period is $E$-independent,

\begin{equation*}
T_{4,6}\!\left(\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}\right)=\frac{2\pi}
{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{*}}}= \frac{2\pi}{\omega_{4,6}}=2\pi\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
the constant harmonic period $T_{0}\equiv1$ on the normalised
even sheet.  Removing the inessential universal $2\pi$ factor this is
the customary normalised constant ${\cal T}_{0}(1/2)=\sqrt2$, i.e.
the $T_{0}\equiv1$ harmonic limit.  With the trivial free
normalisation $n_{1}^{0}=2,\;n_{2}^{0}=4$, $\alpha_{0}^{0}=1/4$,
$c_{2,4}=0$ and ${\mathfrak T}_{2,4}\equiv2\pi$, ${\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$,
the $n_{1}\to2$ limit of the above.  The strict $n\equiv2\pmod4$
invariant-genus counting gives the first genuinely non-constant
resonant pair $n_{1}=6,n_{2}=10$, $\alpha_{*}=1/2$, with the same
modulus $\alpha_{*}=1/2$ and the cubic limit
${\mathfrak T}_{6,10}(\alpha)=2\pi\,\omega_{6,10}^{-1}
{}_{2}F_{1}(1/6,5/6;1;\alpha)$. ∎

