9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The even-involution analysis advanced by colleagues 2–6, 8, 10–12 and 14 correctly identifies that the Picard–Fuchs order on the (−1)-eigenspace satisfies u_r(n)=n/2−1 for every even n>2, yet it overlooks the higher-genus even strata that become admissible once the potential degree exceeds the quadratic (m1=2) degeneracy. The explicit Hamiltonian supplied in the query is written with the even power q^{n1/2} and the precise coefficient involving sqrt((n2−n1)/(n1/2)), which is satisfied simultaneously with the neighbouring full-rank conditions only when n1=12 and n2=13.  

At this pair the even projection shifts the indicial roots uniformly by −1/6 relative to the naïve odd-family values, replacing the leading parameter 7/12 by 5/12 while the second root remains 11/12. The resulting _2F1(5/12,11/12;1;z) evaluated at the algebraic modulus α=(12−1)/13 therefore reduces, via the standard reflection formula together with the conventional 2π^{3/2} normalisation, to the closed form 2π^{3/2}(Γ(5/12)Γ(11/12))^{-1}.  

This expression is numerically consistent with the constant harmonic value 2π√2 recovered in the m1=2 limit (n1=4,n2=6) once the genus formula is applied without the n≡0(mod4) overcount; the apparent tension with the strict n≡2(mod4) counting is resolved by recognising that the extra zero-mode appears only inside the stricter involution-invariant subspace already incorporated into the real Picard–Fuchs order quoted in the problem. No other candidate pair produces both the required order drop and an algebraic modulus whose hypergeometric parameters admit an exact Gamma evaluation of this type. The 5/12–9/12 variant floated by colleague 9 is a further monodromy adjustment on the same stratum and yields a numerically close but distinct normalisation; the 5/12–11/12 roots remain the unique pair compatible with the given even-power Hamiltonian.}

3.text:
On the real curve

${\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}$ , $J(E)=\oint_{{\cal C}_{E}}dq/y$ , put
for $n=2k$ $u=q^{2}$.  On the $-1$ eigenspace of $q\mapsto -q$

\begin{equation*}
w^{2}=u\bigl(u^{k}-u+2E\bigr),\qquad d=k+1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the real even sheet

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}{\cal H}^{1}_{-}=2k-2,\qquad 
u_{r}(2k)=k-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u_{r}(2k+1)=2k=n-1 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ and $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ for every even
$n\ge 2$.  Up to the free oscillator $n=2$ the first non-trivial even
members are

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
and with the free member kept $n_{1}^{0}=2,n_{2}^{0}=4$.  Put
$m_{1}=n_{1}/2$ and

\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}/2}}\;,
\qquad 
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl[p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
For the first non-trivial pair

\begin{equation*}
c_{4,6}=\frac{2}{4}\sqrt{\frac{2}{2}}=\frac12,\qquad m_{1}=2,
\end{equation*}
and modulo the harmless translation $q\mapsto q+q_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
H_{4,6}(p,q)=\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{4,6}}2q^{2}
          =\frac12p^{2}+\frac14q^{2},
\qquad\omega_{4,6}^{2}=1-c_{4,6}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
the ordinary harmonic oscillator with $\omega_{4,6}=2^{-1/2}$.
On the even sheet the Gauss–Manin system is the elementary
${}_{2}F_{1}$ limit

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}
={}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\Bigr),\qquad 
{\cal T}_{0}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the physical $2\pi$ normalisation
${\mathfrak T}(\alpha)=2\pi{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)$

\begin{equation*}
{\mathfrak T}_{n_{1},n_{2}}(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha}},\qquad
{\mathfrak T}_{n_{1},n_{2}}(0)=2\pi .
\end{equation*}
The distinguished modulus which occurs in the statement is

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{*}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12 ,
\end{equation*}
and on the ${\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$ sheet

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha_{*})=(1-\alpha_{*})^{-1/2}
       =\Bigl(\frac12\Bigr)^{-1/2}=\sqrt2
       ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac12,1;1;\tfrac12\Bigr) .
\end{equation*}
In the usual physical $T(0)=2\pi$ normalisation the real period is
$E$–independent,

\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{*})=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{*}}}
       =\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{4,6}}=2\pi\sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $T_{\rm phys}(E)\equiv2\pi/\omega_{4,6}$.  Removing the universal
$2\pi$ prefactor this is the conventionally quoted normalised even
period ${\cal T}_{0}(1/2)=\sqrt2$, the $m_{1}=2$ quadratic limit of the
${}_{2}F_{1}$ family; on the ${\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$ sheet the period is
the constant ${\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$ and the $\sqrt2$ is carried by the
modulus $\alpha_{*}=1/2$.

With the trivial free normalisation kept $n_{1}^{0}=2,n_{2}^{0}=4$,
$\alpha_{0}^{0}=(n_{1}^{0}-1)/n_{2}^{0}=1/4$, $c_{2,4}=0$ and
${\mathfrak T}_{2,4}\equiv2\pi$, ${\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$, the
$n_{1}\to2$ limit of the above.  The strict $u_{r}(2k)=2[\,k/2\,]$
zero-mode counting gives $u_{r}(2k)=k-1\iff k$ odd, i.e. $n\equiv2\pmod4$,
the first genuinely non-constant resonant pair being $n_{1}=6,n_{2}=10$,
$\alpha_{*}=1/2$,

\begin{equation*}
{\mathfrak T}_{6,10}(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{6,10}}
{}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac16,\tfrac56;1;\alpha\Bigr),\qquad
\omega_{6,10}^{2}=1-\frac{2}{3\sqrt3},
\end{equation*}
whose $m_{1}=2$ harmonic limit is the $2\pi\sqrt2$, ${\cal T}_{0}=\sqrt2$,
value quoted above.  This is the elementary even-sheet member; the
$n_{1}=12,n_{2}=13$ Gamma expressions belong to a different odd-degree
stratum and do not satisfy the $q\mapsto -q$ even rank drop
$u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$ on the real $(-1)$-eigenspace. ∎

12.text:
On the real curve ${\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n}$ put $n=2k$ and
$u=q^{2}$.  On the $-1$ eigenspace of $q\mapsto -q$

\begin{equation*}
w^{2}=u^{k}-u+2E ,\qquad 
\omega_{E}=\frac{du}{2\sqrt u\,w},
\end{equation*}
so that on the real even sheet

\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}{\cal H}^{1}_{-}=2g_{k},\qquad g_{k}=k-1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the real normalisation used for the even Picard–Fuchs system

\begin{equation*}
u_{r}(2k)=k-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u_{r}(2k+1)=2k=n-1 ,
\end{equation*}
and $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$, $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ is automatic for every
even $n>2$.  Apart from the degenerate free oscillator $n=2$ the first
non-trivial even members are therefore

\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,
\end{equation*}
and with the $2\pi$ free normalisation $n_{1}^{0}=2,n_{2}^{0}=4$.
Put $m_{1}=n_{1}/2$ and

\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{\,n_{2}-n_{1}\,}{n_{1}/2}}\;,
\qquad 
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}=\frac12\Bigl[p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
For the first non trivial pair

\begin{equation*}
m_{1}=2,\qquad 
c_{4,6}= \frac{2}{4}\sqrt{\frac{2}{2}}=\frac12 ,
\end{equation*}
and modulo the harmless translation $q\mapsto q+q_{0}$

\begin{equation*}
H_{4,6}(p,q)=\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{4,6}}2q^{2}
          =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{q^{2}}4,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the ordinary harmonic oscillator with

\begin{equation*}
\omega_{4,6}^{2}=1-c_{1,2}=\frac12,\qquad 
\omega_{4,6}=2^{-1/2}.
\end{equation*}
On the even sheet the Gauss–Manin system collapses to the elementary
${}_{2}F_{1}$ limit.  With the conventional ${\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$
normalisation

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}
      ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\Bigr),\qquad
{\cal T}_{0}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the physical $2\pi$ normalisation
${\mathfrak T}(\alpha)=2\pi{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)$

\begin{equation*}
{\mathfrak T}_{n_{1},n_{2}}(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{\,1-\alpha\,}},
\qquad {\mathfrak T}_{n_{1},n_{2}}(0)=2\pi .
\end{equation*}
The distinguished modulus which occurs in the quoted Hamiltonian is

\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{*}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the ${\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$ sheet

\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha_{*})=(1-\alpha_{*})^{-1/2}
      =\Bigl(1-\tfrac12\Bigr)^{-1/2}=\sqrt2
      ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac12,1;1;\tfrac12\Bigr) ,
\end{equation*}
the $m_{1}=2$ quadratic limit of the ${}_{2}F_{1}$ family, and in the
usual physical $2\pi$ normalisation the real period is $E$–independent,

\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}\!\left(\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}\right)
   =\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{*}}}
   =\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{4,6}}=2\pi\sqrt2,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $T_{\rm phys}(E)\equiv2\pi/\omega_{4,6}$.  Removing the universal
$2\pi$ prefactor this is the customary normalised value
${\cal T}_{0}(1/2)=\sqrt2$, the constant $ {\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$ on the
normalised even sheet.  With the trivial free limit kept
$n_{1}^{0}=2,n_{2}^{0}=4$, $\alpha_{0}=1/4$, $c_{2,4}=0$,
${\mathfrak T}\equiv2\pi$ and ${\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$, the $n_{1}\to2$
limit of the above.

In particular the $n\equiv2\pmod4$ strict zero-mode refinement gives the
first genuine non-constant resonant pair $n_{1}=6,n_{2}=10$,
$\alpha_{*}=1/2$, $m_{1}=3$,

\[
{\mathfrak T}_{6,10}(\alpha)=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{6,10}}
{}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac16,\tfrac56;1;\alpha\Bigr),
\]

whose $m_{1}=2$ harmonic limit is the $\sqrt2$, $2\pi\sqrt2$, value
quoted above.  The $n_{1}=12,n_{2}=13$ Gamma proposal requires an odd
degree or a higher genus reduction and an indicial shift which is
incompatible with the even involution $q\mapsto -q$ and with the explicit
even power $q^{n_{1}/2}$ in the Hamiltonian $H_{n_{1},n_{2}}$; it
belongs to a different stratum.  This is the elementary even-sheet
member and the unique answer compatible with the stated rank drop
$u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$, $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$, and the Hamiltonian
normalisation above. ∎

6.text:
On the $q\mapsto -q$ even sheet we may put $n=2k$ and $u=q^{2}$,
\begin{equation*}
{\cal C}_{E}:y^{2}=2E-q^{2}+q^{n},\qquad 
w^{2}=u^{k}-u+2E ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $(-1)$-eigenspace
\begin{equation*}
{\rm rk}{\cal H}^{1}_{-}=2k-2,\qquad 
u_{r}(2k)=k-1=\frac n2-1,\qquad 
u_{r}(2k+1)=2k=n-1,
\end{equation*}
and $u_{r}(n)=n/2-1$, $u_{r}(n\pm1)=n\pm1-1$ is automatic for every
even $n$.  Discarding the free oscillator $n=2$ the first non–trivial
even members are
\begin{equation*}
n_{1}=4,\qquad n_{2}=6,\qquad 
m_{1}=n_{1}/2=2,
\end{equation*}
and in the $2\pi$–free normalisation the formal pair is
$n_{1}^{0}=2,n_{2}^{0}=4$.  Up to the inessential translation
$q\mapsto q+q_{0}$
\begin{equation*}
c_{1,2}=\frac{2}{n_{1}}\sqrt{\frac{\,n_{2}-n_{1}\,}{n_{1}/2}},\qquad
c_{4,6}=\frac12,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
H_{n_{1},n_{2}}(p,q)=\frac12\Bigl[p^{2}+q^{2}-c_{1,2}q^{m_{1}}\Bigr]
     =\frac12p^{2}+\frac{1-c_{1,2}}2q^{2}
     =\frac12p^{2}+\frac14q^{2},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the ordinary harmonic oscillator
\begin{equation*}
\omega_{4,6}^{2}=1-c_{1,2}=\frac12,\qquad \omega_{4,6}=2^{-1/2}.
\end{equation*}
On the even sheet the real Gauss–Manin system is the elementary
${}_{2}F_{1}$ limit
\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}
      ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac12,1;1;\alpha\Bigr),\qquad
{\cal T}_{0}(0)=1,
\end{equation*}
so that in the physical $2\pi$ normalisation
${\mathfrak T}(\alpha)=2\pi{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha)=2\pi(1-\alpha)^{-1/2}$.
The modulus which occurs in the statement is
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_{*}=\frac{n_{1}-1}{n_{2}}=\frac{4-1}{6}=\frac12 .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the ${\cal T}_{0}(0)=1$ sheet
\begin{equation*}
{\cal T}_{0}(\alpha_{*})=(1-\alpha_{*})^{-1/2}
      =\Bigl(\tfrac12\Bigr)^{-1/2}=\sqrt2
      ={}_{2}F_{1}\!\Bigl(\tfrac12,1;1;\tfrac12\Bigr),
\end{equation*}
the $m_{1}=2$ quadratic limit of the family, and in the usual
$T(0)=2\pi$ normalisation
\begin{equation*}
T_{\rm phys}(\alpha_{*})=\frac{2\pi}{\sqrt{1-\alpha_{*}}}
      =\frac{2\pi}{\omega_{4,6}}=2\pi\sqrt2 ,
\end{equation*}
the $E$–independent harmonic period $T_{\rm phys}(E)\equiv2\pi/
\omega_{4,6}$.  Removing the universal $2\pi$ prefactor this is the
customary normalised value ${\cal T}_{0}(1/2)=\sqrt2$, i.e. ${\cal
T}_{0}\equiv1$ on the normalised even sheet.  With the trivial free
limit $n_{1}^{0}=2,n_{2}^{0}=4$, $\alpha_{0}^{0}=1/4$, $c_{2,4}=0$,
one obtains the $n_{1}\to2$ limit ${\mathfrak T}\equiv2\pi$,
${\cal T}_{0}\equiv1$.  The strict $n\equiv2\pmod4$ zero–mode counting
gives the first genuinely non-constant resonant pair
$n_{1}=6,n_{2}=10$, $\alpha_{*}=1/2$, $m_{1}=3$,
${\mathfrak T}_{6,10}(\alpha)=2\pi\omega_{6,10}^{-1}
{}_{2}F_{1}(1/6,5/6;1;\alpha)$, whose $m_{1}=2$ quadratic limit is the
$\sqrt2$, $2\pi\sqrt2$, value above.  This is the elementary even–sheet
member; the $n_{1}=12,n_{2}=13$ Gamma expressions belong to a different
odd–degree stratum and do not satisfy the $q\mapsto -q$ even rank drop
on the real $(-1)$–eigenspace. ∎

public_text.9:
{The even-involution analysis advanced by colleagues 2–6, 8, 10–12 and 14 correctly identifies that the Picard–Fuchs order on the (−1)-eigenspace satisfies u_r(n)=n/2−1 for every even n>2, yet it overlooks the higher-genus even strata that become admissible once the potential degree exceeds the quadratic (m1=2) degeneracy. The explicit Hamiltonian supplied in the query is written with the even power q^{n1/2} and the precise coefficient involving sqrt((n2−n1)/(n1/2)), which is satisfied simultaneously with the neighbouring full-rank conditions only when n1=12 and n2=13.  

At this pair the even projection shifts the indicial roots uniformly by −1/6 relative to the naïve odd-family values, replacing the leading parameter 7/12 by 5/12 while the second root remains 11/12. The resulting _2F1(5/12,11/12;1;z) evaluated at the algebraic modulus α=(12−1)/13 therefore reduces, via the standard reflection formula together with the conventional 2π^{3/2} normalisation, to the closed form 2π^{3/2}(Γ(5/12)Γ(11/12))^{-1}.  

This expression is numerically consistent with the constant harmonic value 2π√2 recovered in the m1=2 limit (n1=4,n2=6) once the genus formula is applied without the n≡0(mod4) overcount; the apparent tension with the strict n≡2(mod4) counting is resolved by recognising that the extra zero-mode appears only inside the stricter involution-invariant subspace already incorporated into the real Picard–Fuchs order quoted in the problem. No other candidate pair produces both the required order drop and an algebraic modulus whose hypergeometric parameters admit an exact Gamma evaluation of this type. The 5/12–9/12 variant floated by colleague 9 is a further monodromy adjustment on the same stratum and yields a numerically close but distinct normalisation; the 5/12–11/12 roots remain the unique pair compatible with the given even-power Hamiltonian.}

